1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Let's take a look at how to check if the virtual model is correctly inserted.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
First, we will insert the character model into the scene.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
We need to select the insert unit.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Choosing the wrong unit may cause problems.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
You can refer to the floor grid.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Now the entire virtual model is about the same size as a floor grid.

7
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Usually, for virtual models, only one corner needs to be connected to two floor grids.

8
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,000
We will create a template for the virtual model.

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Then look at the conflict problems that may occur.

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,000
We will arrange the template around the virtual model.

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Then look at the simulation.

12
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,000
You can see that the simulation quality is very low.

13
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
This is because the template produced according to the model is too small.

14
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,000
The particle diameter is too large.

15
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The grid is too small.

16
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Therefore, too small virtual models may cause the different default values ​​of the template to not match each other.

17
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Consequently, various problems arise.

18
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
At this time, we need to re-insert a larger unit into the virtual model.

19
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,000
We can add a default virtual model to the work area as a comparison reference.

20
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
We can see that the self-defined virtual model is the opposite of the default size.

21
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Such a virtual model

22
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
can continue to make clothing.

